🧩 EBS Scenario-Based Interview Questions and Answers
1️⃣ Scenario: Your EC2 instance is running out of disk space. How do you increase the EBS volume size without downtime?
Answer:
Stop or keep the instance running (EBS supports live resize).
In AWS Console → EC2 → Volumes, choose “Modify Volume.”
Increase the volume size (e.g., 30 GB → 50 GB).
Wait until the volume state becomes “optimizing.”
Resize the filesystem inside the instance:

For Linux:
sudo growpart /dev/xvda 1
sudo resize2fs /dev/xvda1

For Windows: use Disk Management → Extend Volume.

✅ No downtime needed for EBS-based root or data volumes (gp2/gp3/io1/io2).

2️⃣ Scenario: An EBS volume was accidentally detached from an EC2 instance. How do you reattach it safely?
Answer:
Go to EC2 → Volumes → select the detached volume.
Choose Attach Volume → select the target EC2 instance.
Note the device name (e.g., /dev/xvdf).
On EC2, verify it:
lsblk
sudo file -s /dev/xvdf
Mount it:
sudo mkdir /data
sudo mount /dev/xvdf /data
Best Practice:
Always label and document volume–instance mappings for quick recovery.

3️⃣ Scenario: You terminated an EC2 instance but lost the attached data. Why and how can you prevent this next time?

Answer:
By default, the root EBS volume of an EC2 instance is deleted upon termination.
You can prevent this by disabling “Delete on termination” at creation or after launch.
Fix for next time:
aws ec2 modify-instance-attribute \
  --instance-id i-0123456789abcdef \
  --block-device-mappings '[{"DeviceName":"/dev/xvda","Ebs":{"DeleteOnTermination":false}}]'

4️⃣ Scenario: How do you back up an EBS volume automatically every day?
Answer:
Use Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM):
Go to EC2 → Lifecycle Manager → Create Policy.
Choose resource type: EBS Volume or Instance.
Define backup schedule (e.g., daily at 12 AM).
Retain snapshots (e.g., keep last 7 backups).
Apply tags like Backup=true to identify volumes.

Alternative: Automate via a Lambda + CloudWatch Event that calls:
aws ec2 create-snapshot --volume-id vol-xxxx --description "Daily backup"

5️⃣ Scenario: How do you restore data from an old EBS snapshot?

Answer:
Go to Snapshots → select the snapshot.
Choose Create Volume from Snapshot → pick the same AZ.
Attach the new volume to any EC2 instance.
Mount and access files.
Optional: Copy snapshots across regions for DR setup.

6️⃣ Scenario: You’re asked to move an EBS volume from one region to another.
Answer:
EBS volumes are AZ-specific, but you can move them via snapshots:
Take a snapshot of the volume.
Copy the snapshot to the target region:
aws ec2 copy-snapshot --source-region us-east-1 \
--source-snapshot-id snap-12345 --destination-region eu-west-1
Create a new volume from the copied snapshot in the destination region.

7️⃣ Scenario: How do you migrate an EBS volume between AZs in the same region?

Answer:
Same method as above:
Take a snapshot of the volume.
Create a new volume in the target AZ from that snapshot.
Detach old volume → attach new one to instance in the new AZ.

8️⃣ Scenario: Your EC2 instance performance is slow. How do you verify if EBS is the bottleneck?
Answer:
Check CloudWatch metrics for:
VolumeReadOps, VolumeWriteOps
VolumeQueueLength (if > 1 for long periods → I/O bottleneck)
VolumeThroughputPercentage
Use iostat or dstat inside instance to check disk I/O usage.
Fix:
Switch to faster volume type (gp3 → io1 or io2).
Enable EBS-optimized instance type.
Use Provisioned IOPS (io1/io2) for consistent latency.

9️⃣ Scenario: You accidentally deleted an EBS volume but need the data. Can it be recovered?
Answer:
❌ No direct recovery once deleted.
✅ However, if you had a snapshot, you can restore from it.
Snapshots are incremental and stored in S3 (durable and recoverable).
Best Practice: Automate periodic snapshots of critical volumes.

🔟 Scenario: How do you encrypt an existing unencrypted EBS volume?
Answer:
EBS encryption cannot be enabled in-place — but you can create an encrypted copy.
Steps:
Create a snapshot of the unencrypted volume.
Copy the snapshot and enable encryption:
aws ec2 copy-snapshot --source-snapshot-id snap-123 --encrypted
Create a new volume from this encrypted snapshot.
Detach old volume and attach the new encrypted one.

11️⃣ Scenario: You want to share an EBS snapshot with another AWS account.
Answer:
Modify snapshot permissions:
aws ec2 modify-snapshot-attribute \
  --snapshot-id snap-12345 \
  --attribute createVolumePermission \
  --operation-type add \
  --user-ids 987654321000
The target account can now create a volume from that snapshot.
(For encrypted snapshots, share the KMS key permissions too.)

12️⃣ Scenario: You notice your snapshots are taking up too much storage. Why?

Answer:
Snapshots are incremental, but deleting a parent snapshot can cause AWS to merge data, increasing size temporarily.
Also, if you take too many unnecessary snapshots, even small deltas add cost.
Fix:
Use Lifecycle Policies to automatically delete older snapshots (e.g., retain only last 7).

13️⃣ Scenario: You created a new gp3 volume, but performance is low. Why?
Answer:
gp3 volumes have default IOPS = 3000 and throughput = 125 MB/s.
You must manually configure higher IOPS/throughput if needed:
aws ec2 modify-volume --volume-id vol-xxxx --iops 6000 --throughput 250

14️⃣ Scenario: Your application writes large sequential files (e.g., logs). Which EBS type should you choose?
Answer:
Use st1 (Throughput Optimized HDD) for large, sequential workloads (e.g., logs, data warehouses).
For small random I/O (databases, apps) → gp3 or io2.

15️⃣ Scenario: You have multiple EBS volumes and want to combine them into one large logical disk.
Answer:
Use LVM (Logical Volume Manager):
Attach all volumes.
Create physical volumes:
sudo pvcreate /dev/xvdf /dev/xvdg
Create volume group and logical volume.
Format and mount.
⚠️ LVM can increase size, but does not increase IOPS beyond combined total.

16️⃣ Scenario: You want to automatically delete EBS volumes that are not attached to any EC2 instance.
Answer:
Use AWS Lambda + EventBridge rule or run periodically:
aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available \
--query "Volumes[*].VolumeId" --output text | \
xargs -n1 aws ec2 delete-volume --volume-id

17️⃣ Scenario: You need to ensure that all new EBS volumes are encrypted by default.
nswer:
Enable EBS encryption by default:
Go to EC2 → Settings → EBS Encryption → Enable
Or via CLI:
aws ec2 enable-ebs-encryption-by-default
All new volumes/snapshots created after that will be encrypted automatically.

18️⃣ Scenario: You need high IOPS (50,000+) for a production database. What’s your solution?

Answer:
Use io2 Block Express volumes — support up to 64 TB and 256,000 IOPS.
Attach to Nitro-based instances (e.g., m5n, r5b, c6i).
Set IOPS explicitly during creation.

19️⃣ Scenario: You want to track changes or usage patterns of all EBS volumes in your account.
Answer:
Use AWS Config to track volume creation/deletion/modifications.
Use CloudWatch metrics for performance.
Use Trusted Advisor → “Underutilized EBS volumes” check.

20️⃣ Scenario: You want to simulate EBS volume failure for testing DR.
Answer:
Stop the EC2 instance.
Detach the volume.
Attach a “corrupted” or blank volume.
Test recovery via restoring snapshot → re-attach restored volume → restart instance.

This helps validate disaster recovery and backup integrity.
